1|0@cripplingalcoholism^1439058144#ctw3yjd:lovelybone93$:Eh, my reading material consists of historical and socio-economic texts and some fiction. I *can* read Lenin's *State and Revolution* on Marxists.org, but I'd rather have it in book form. It's not hard to grasp either. I love reading, it's liberating.
1|0@cripplingalcoholism^1439059356#ctw4le8:Pine_Forest_Dweller$:That's pretty cool! I think it is too. Sometimes, I feel like I've lost some of my creativity (I used to paint and draw) when I got on medication and I enjoy using my imagination when I read. I do some photography now but am in no way a professional. When I read, I love to create the scenes and situations in my mind and try to figure out what will happen ahead of time. It makes me feel creative. I like auto-biographies and fiction, mostly. I go through phases. I've read a lot of books on addiction and alcoholism from auto-biographies to self-help. Lots of self-help books. Right now I'm reading "To Kill a Mockingbird" again so I can read Harper Lee's new manuscript, "Go Set a Watchman." I like the classics too. Loved "The Grapes of Wrath," and "Catcher in the Rye." High school reading that I missed out on. I also like some poetry, I love Kahil Gibran, he's my favorite. "The Prophet"... That book got me through many a dark night. I've read a lot of Scott Fitzgerald and Patricia Highsmith but I would have to re-read them to tell you anything about them. My memory sucks but at least I get to re-read all my books and enjoy them more than once! Some things I remember more than others. My mother reads a lot too and she prefers the hand-held books like you. She says she needs to be able to turn the pages. Sorry to ramble like this, but you drew it out of me. It's not my fault. :-)
1|0@cripplingalcoholism^1439060066#ctw4y5j:lovelybone93$:I'm not a particularly creative person myself, but for others it takes the right spark. I read fantasy and historical fiction. Just got finished reading *Gotrek and Felix: The First Omnibus* that a friend sent me (enthralling as fuck), but my favorite fiction book has to be Alexandre Dumas' *The Count of Monte Cristo*, or Cervantes' *Don Quijote de la Mancha*. For a humorous and easily read novel, try J.P. Donleavy's *The Gingerbread Man*. It's no problem talking with a fellow bibliophile.
